{0 Reference Verification Architecture}

This page records the intended separation between the semantic verification
kernel and implementation choices such as sharing, slicing, batching, dumps,
Why3 emission, SMT calls, and profiling.

The dependency-layer check described in [docs/architecture_layer_rules.md] is
necessary but not sufficient. It verifies that libraries point in the right
direction. It does not say which transformations are part of the correctness
argument and which transformations are only representation or backend
optimizations.

{1 Architecture Type}

Kairos uses a layered scientific domain with a concrete engine:

- incoming adapters parse and elaborate the source language;
- the domain core contains the elaborated language and shared IR datatypes;
- the verification domain consumes supplied automata to construct product proof
  data;
- [Step_contract_projection] and [Proof_plan] expose the active
  implementation obligations and prover-independent planning;
- outgoing adapters render artifacts or call external tools;
- [Engine_flow] coordinates the single concrete frontend and runtime services.

The semantic boundary is explicit in the active data path; no unused exchange
view or single-instance port layer is required to represent it.

{1 Reference Boundary}

The essential reference boundary starts after frontend elaboration and after
the property automata have been supplied:

{[
Verification_model.program_model
  + supplied assumption and guarantee automata
  -> product states
  -> product steps
  -> generated source/destination obligations
]}

The [Verification_model.program_model] at this boundary is the normalized core
model. [Verification_model.normalize_node_semantics] is the named entry point:
it applies source-order transition priority and makes the implicit default
skip explicit as a generated self-loop step. This corresponds to the source
step principle formalized in Rocq, without forcing the OCaml implementation to
reuse Rocq's [Real]/[Default] representation.

This path must be deterministic and independent from:

- Spot invocation;
- Why3 or Z3;
- backend grouping thresholds;
- textual WhyML size optimizations;
- worker scheduling;
- dump generation;
- profiling counters.

Spot may be used to produce automata, but the essential boundary consumes
automata as parameters. Rocq adequacy should therefore be checked relative to
the supplied automata and the generated product obligations, not with Spot
itself or with a formalization of the LTL-to-automata translation.
The reference product is still responsible for validating the automata normal
form it consumes: non-empty automata, well-formed transition indices,
at most one bad state per automaton, non-bad initial states, an absorbing
assumption bad state, and the assumption-edge determinism needed by canonical
summary identity. This validation rejects malformed supplied automata; it does
not prove monitor correctness for the source temporal formulas.

{1 Rocq Adequacy Boundary}

The Rocq development is not derived from the OCaml implementation. It already
captures the mathematical core. The OCaml architecture must therefore expose
which pieces belong to the corresponding essential boundary and which pieces
are only extensions, exchange projections, optimizations, or backends.

The Rocq source used for alignment is recorded in
[docs/rocq_alignment_manifest.json]. The central theorem cut is:

{[
GeneratedObligationsValid
  -> contract_valid
]}

This is the theorem shape the paper should present mathematically. Kairos must
make the implementation artifacts behind [GeneratedObligationsValid]
inspectable and stable, while the concrete instance exposes
[concrete_environment_generated_obligations_sound].

The manifest records this with [rocq_scope]:

- {b essential}: semantic stages whose meaning must match the Rocq core;
- {b extension}: generated invariant or obligation schemes that require a
  general semantic principle if used;
- {b projection}: exchange or diagnostic views derived from the boundary;
- {b excluded}: frontend, external tools, optimizations, backends, and
  presentation.

This prevents the wrong direction of work: OCaml modules do not prescribe a
new Rocq theorem inventory. They must be organized so the existing
formalization can be compared with the essential path.

{1 Current Pipeline Map}

The current implementation roughly has the following stages:

- surface parsing and elaboration:
  [Kx_surface_syntax], [Kx_elaborate], [Kx_ast], [Kairos_to_model];
- source well-formedness frontiers:
  [Historical_initialization] and [Kairos_to_model_node_validation] check that
  formulas using [pre]/[pre_k] have enough available history at their source
  point;
- core semantic model:
  [Verification_model], [Core_syntax];
- automata construction:
  [Automata_generation] in [kairos_runtime_automata], wired to Spot from
  runtime orchestration;
- reference product construction:
  [Product_build], [Temporal_automata], [From_model],
  [Orchestration.build_reference_product];
- proof/IR shaping passes:
  [Pre], [Product_reachability], [Post], [Temporal_lower];
- proof-contract construction and planning:
  [Step_contract_projection], [Contract_formula_index], [Proof_plan];
- runtime/backend orchestration:
  [Pipeline_build] and [Runtime_snapshot] in [kairos_runtime_core],
  external automata production in [kairos_runtime_automata], [Proof_runner]
  in [kairos_runtime_proof], diagnostics in [kairos_runtime_diagnostics],
  and direct orchestration behind the public [Kairos_engine.Api] facade;
- Why3-specific representation and emission:
  [Why_compile], [Why_compile_step], [Why_pipeline].

[Ir.node_ir] and the formula-bearing summary types are indexed by their
history capability. Before [Temporal_lower], the reference pipeline carries
[Core_syntax.historical] formulas. [Temporal_lower] is the unique typed
boundary to [Core_syntax.history_free] formulas. The Why3 backend only accepts
the history-free form, so an [HPreK] case cannot reach its emitter.

The current technical debt is that some stages named as domain passes still
contain representation or cost decisions, and some backend views perform
simplifications rather than only projecting data to Why3.

{1 Pass Classification}

Every pass should be classified before it is moved or optimized:

- {b reference}: part of the essential semantic construction whose adequacy
  must be checked against Rocq;
- {b reference_extension}: generated invariant or obligation scheme that is
  correction-relevant, but should be justified by a general semantic
  principle rather than baked into the minimal core;
- {b reference_normalization}: deterministic semantic normalization required
  by the reference view;
- {b reference_projection}: derived exchange or diagnostic view, not the
  semantic source of obligations;
- {b obligation-preserving optimization}: grouping, sharing, slicing, or cuts
  that must preserve generated obligations;
- {b backend}: target-specific representation, command invocation, proof
  result collection, and reporting;
- {b external}: Spot, Why3, Z3, Graphviz, timing services, and filesystem
  artifact generation.

The rule is:

{[
reference output is stable under backend options
]}

Changing a sharing threshold, worker count, dump option, or Why3 grouping mode
must not change the canonical product steps or active proof contracts.

{2 Current Classification}

The current pass classification is:

- [Pre] is {b reference}. It adds [propagation_requires], local [requires],
  assumption/program guard requirements, stability requirements, and initial
  invariant goals. Product-entry facts must satisfy the [Fo_current_input]
  current-input-free predicate: any current input needed as an end-of-instant
  fact must have been shifted to a historical occurrence first. These facts
  shape the proof obligations for a product step.
- [Product_reachability] is {b reference_extension}. Despite its name, it
  must not be treated as pruning. The current pass adds obligations such as
  [guard => false] for destinations classified as unreachable by the finite
  analysis. The essential proof story should see the generic invariant
  principle; this synthesis is one implementation-side extension of that
  principle.
- [Historical_initialization] is {b reference_normalization}. It is the
  implementation-side witness for Rocq [InitializationFrontier]: a formula is
  accepted at a source point only if its required historical depth is bounded
  by the age available at that point. The OCaml computation
  [min_ticks_by_state] is not certified by Rocq; it is the current frontend
  algorithm for producing the ages used by the validator. This check may
  reject source programs, but it must not rewrite, drop, or weaken proof
  obligations.
- [Post] is {b reference}. It adds the safe-successor disjunction, destination
  invariant obligations, guarded destination invariant obligations, and
  product-characteristic preservation facts.
- [Temporal_lower] is {b reference_normalization}. It computes the
  [temporal_layout], rewrites [pre]/[pre_k] occurrences through explicit typed
  bindings and interns every location-free lowered result. It is part of the
  active implementation path, not a Why3-specific copy.
- [Step_contract_projection] is {b reference}. It constructs the proof
  contracts actually consumed by the implementation directly from lowered
  [Ir.product_step_summary] values.
- [Proof_plan] is {b obligation_preserving_optimization}. It attaches
  partition provenance and makes prover-independent grouping, factorization
  and sharing decisions before any Why3 term exists.

The machine-readable version of this classification is
[docs/reference_pipeline_boundaries.json]. The check
[scripts/check_reference_pipeline_boundaries.py] rejects missing stages,
unknown classification names, stages left as [to_classify], and references to
unknown Rocq alignment units.

{2 Rocq Alignment Cut}

The current Rocq alignment manifest compares the following semantic objects:

- source language and well-formed programs;
- instrumented runs and observations;
- historical-initialization frontier for source formulas using [pre]/[pre_k];
- supplied contract automata;
- product anchors and classification;
- the active [Ir.product_step_summary] values;
- step-contract construction by [Step_contract_projection];
- prover-independent planning by [Proof_plan];
- generated safe, unsafe, and initialization obligation validity;
- soundness assembly.

POPL PaperCore fixes the Rocq proof claims, while the active OCaml pipeline
fixes the implementation architecture. The comparison is semantic: the
internal stages and records of the Rocq proof do not require matching OCaml
modules. The current direct comparison is recorded in
{!page-rocq_ocaml_adequacy}.

{1 Rigorous Architecture Modeling}

Architecture should be represented at three complementary levels.

First, use diagrams for orientation, not as the source of truth:

- a C4-style context diagram for users, Kairos, Rocq, Spot, Why3, and Z3;
- a container/component diagram for frontend, domain core, verification
  kernel, runtime orchestration, and backends;
- a pipeline diagram showing data artifacts and pass boundaries.

Second, keep a versioned manifest as the source of truth for architecture
classification. The manifest should record:

- stage name;
- classification vocabulary;
- owned paths;
- inputs and outputs;
- allowed effects;
- forbidden effects;
- Rocq adequacy role.

Third, enforce the manifest with scripts. Checks should fail when:

- a reference stage imports or mentions external-tool adapters;
- a pass remains unclassified;
- a path in the manifest disappears;
- backend options affect canonical kernel output;
- a reference pass starts deleting product cases without an explicit certified
  reachability argument.

The current regression for the fourth point is
[tests/check_reference_stability.sh]. It compares the normalized program and
proof IR dumps produced for the same source program under different
proof-planning and runtime options. In particular, disabling step-contract
grouping may change the completed proof plan, but must not change either
reference view. Source-level transformations such as public guarantee grouping
remain outside this comparison.

For design history, use short Architecture Decision Records. An ADR should
record one decision, its alternatives, and the invariant that must survive
future refactors. The manifest says what the architecture is; ADRs say why it
became that way.

{1 Active Obligation Boundary}

The implementation exposes its proof content through
[Ir.product_step_summary], [Step_contract_projection.step_contract] and
[Proof_plan.t]. These are active objects, not a serialization contract
invented for Rocq synchronization.

Any future exchange format must be derived from these objects and justified by
an actual consumer. Serialization alone is not an adequacy proof.

{1 Optimization Discipline}

Optimizations should live after the reference kernel, or be marked explicitly
as normalization with a simple proof obligation.

Acceptable optimization shapes include:

- replacing repeated formulas by definitions;
- simplifying first-order formulas by equivalence-preserving rewrites;
- deduplicating identical Why3 terms;
- slicing an imperative transition body from the variables needed by the
  selected obligations, without changing those obligations;
- grouping conclusions that already share the same source precondition;
- factoring grouped formulas into a logically equivalent shape;
- choosing a backend-specific representation for already generated facts;
- scheduling independent obligations in parallel.

The following are not optimizations unless justified by a separate certified
argument:

- deleting product edges;
- filtering mixed-phase guards by syntactic contradiction;
- shifting program guards or assumption guards into later ticks;
- hiding reachability assumptions in the backend;
- changing generated product obligations because a backend cannot prove them.

The phase discipline behind these rules is described in
{!page-temporal_phase_semantics}. The current product-proof status is recorded
in {!page-product_proof_pipeline_audit}. The performance motivation for
sharing and grouping is recorded in {!page-pipeline_cost_model}. The boundary
needed for later Rocq adequacy checking is recorded in
{!page-rocq_proof_scope}.

{2 Why3 Product Backend Boundary}

The backend boundary is the private, backend-independent [Proof_plan.t].
[Pipeline_build] builds that plan once and stores it in the runtime snapshot.
Why3 generation and goal attribution consume the same value.

- [Step_contract_projection] derives step contracts independently in every
  reference partition, after its own reachability analysis.
- [Contract_formula_index] selects repeated canonical formulas without Why3
  terms.
- [Proof_plan] assembles the source-node view, preserves partition provenance,
  groups compatible safe contracts, factors common preconditions, groups
  identical conclusions, and selects shared postconditions.
- [Why_compile_node_common] translates the planned source signature and
  temporal layout.
- [Why_compile_product_specs] translates planned logical conditions to concrete
  Why3 specifications without comparing or regrouping [Ptree] terms.
- [Why_compile_product_helpers] emits the already selected individual or
  grouped helper.
- [Why_compile_step] translates [Ir.transition] and [Core_syntax.stmt].
- [Why_compile] orders these translations and builds the manifest.

The output types of [Proof_plan] are private: a backend can inspect the
completed plan but cannot construct inconsistent sharing identifiers or
factored obligations. [Why_compile_formula_sharing] and
[Why_compile_bundles] only materialize decisions present in the plan.

The permitted data flow is:

{[
partition-local history-free IR
  -> step-contract projection
  -> backend-independent proof plan
  -> mechanical Why3 translation
]}

{1 Maintenance Rule}

1. Keep the active implementation path visible in the reference-boundary
   manifest.
2. Justify semantic transformations on the objects the pipeline actually
   consumes.
3. Keep Why3 translation, profiling and dumps outside obligation construction.
4. Compare the active objects with POPL semantically; do not recreate unused
   OCaml mirrors of Rocq proof records.
